<RaiseFault name="RF-PromptBlocked">
  <DisplayName>400 — prompt blocked by Model Armor</DisplayName>
  <!--
    THIS POLICY IS WHAT KEEPS MODEL ARMOR AN ENFORCEMENT CONTROL.

    SUP-SanitizeUserPrompt runs with continueOnError="true" so that a Model
    Armor OUTAGE does not kill a real user's LLM call (the fail-open decision,
    2026-09-15). But Apigee delivers a genuine content match through the SAME
    channel as an infrastructure failure: a detected prompt injection or
    jailbreak is raised as a policy error,
      HTTP 400 steps.sanitize.user.prompt.FilterMatched
    (docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/.../sanitize-user-prompt-policy).

    So continueOnError="true" on its own does NOT mean "tolerate outages". It
    means "swallow the match faults too", i.e. flagged prompts sail through to
    Vertex and Model Armor silently degrades to logging-only. This step exists
    to re-raise that block explicitly, from the verdict variable rather than
    from the swallowed fault.

    The split it creates, which is the whole point:
      filterMatchState = MATCH_FOUND  -> real violation -> block here (fail closed)
      policy failed, no verdict       -> Model Armor unreachable -> proceed
                                         unscanned, recorded by AM-ScanVerdict
                                         (fail open)

    UNVERIFIED ASSUMPTION — CONFIRM BEFORE TRUSTING THIS. This depends on
    filterMatchState still being populated on the FilterMatched error path.
    The variable name itself is confirmed (debug session 2026-09-15, see
    AM-ScanVerdict.xml), but it was confirmed on a CLEAN call, not on a blocked
    one. If Apigee skips the assignment when it raises FilterMatched, this
    condition never fires and flagged prompts pass. That must be proven with a
    deliberately malicious prompt in a debug session before this proxy is
    relied on for enforcement. Until then, treat prompt blocking as UNPROVEN.
  -->
  <FaultResponse>
    <Set>
      <StatusCode>400</StatusCode>
      <ReasonPhrase>Bad Request</ReasonPhrase>
      <Payload contentType="application/json">{"error":"prompt_blocked","reason":"content_policy"}</Payload>
    </Set>
  </FaultResponse>
  <IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>true</IgnoreUnresolvedVariables>
</RaiseFault>
